{
 "_about": "Check-control ids (cCIdentifier / ccmId / CC-ID) to meanings, from PUBLIC sources only, package P 2026-11-16. Meanings are paraphrased in our own words, never BMW's message text. 'g20' true = the id is tied to a G-series car (G20 among the models) by a BMW service bulletin published on nhtsa.gov; false = the number comes from a community list compiled on E/F-series cars and is UNVERIFIED on the G20 (the numbering is BMW-wide and SIB 34 08 21 shows id 42 kept its meaning, but at least id 49 changed). 'safety' = our classification (see _safety). 'symbol' / 'marker' = the pictogram of design/strip/pictograms we would draw; null = none of ours fits yet.",
 "_symbolId": "cCSymbolID (CCMessageStruct, UInt16) and cCListSymbolID (CCListElementStruct, UInt16) have NO enumeration in the car's interface catalogue and no public mapping was found (searches for the field names return nothing). Two facts from the catalogue (verified, field and type names only): cCMessageWithSymbol carries a KISymbolStruct { symbolID UInt16, symbolFormat PNG|JPEG|RLE24|RLE32, symbolData UInt8[0..65535] } - the head unit can send the pictogram's BITMAP with the message - and KombiInterface_2.requestDefaultSymbols(CC_LIST_SYMBOL | CBS_LIST_SYMBOL | NOTIFICATION_LIST_SYMBOL) returns up to 32 such bitmaps. That bitmap is BMW's artwork: it may be drawn at runtime from the car's own data if the owner ever wants that, but it must never be logged, saved, baked or committed. Until the car's log shows (cCIdentifier, cCSymbolID) pairs, map symbols by cCIdentifier with this table and treat cCSymbolID as unknown.",
 "_colour": "CCColorEnum { DEFAULT 0, RED 1, YELLOW 2, GREEN 3, ORANGE 4, WHITE 5 } (catalogue, verified). BMW manuals: red = stop safely / do not continue; yellow = continue with care, have it checked. 'class' below is the colour a source states; null = not stated.",
 "_safety": "safety true when the message concerns brakes, steering, restraint (belts, airbags), tyres losing pressure, an engine that must be stopped (oil pressure, overheating), the drivetrain stopping, the car rolling away, or a red message. For these the brief's rule is absolute: BMW's panel stays visible unless our own text for it is on the screen in the same frame. UN R121 Table 1 gives the colour classes behind this: seat belt (item 21) red, brake system (25) per R13/R13-H (red), engine malfunction (30) yellow, low tyre pressure (42a) yellow.",
 "sources": {
  "SIB342108": "BMW SIB 34 08 21 (2021-07-23), G20/G22/G23/G29: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2021/MC-10204626-9999.pdf",
  "SIB610921": "BMW SIB 61 09 21 (2021), G20 and others, 48 V battery: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2021/MC-10202471-9999.pdf",
  "SIB120721": "BMW SIB 12 07 21 (rev. 2023), B58 48 V belt slip, G20 and others: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2023/MC-10245367-0001.pdf",
  "SIB620819": "BMW SIB 62 08 19 (2019), G20 and others, range zero: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2019/MC-10164011-9999.pdf",
  "SIB611621": "BMW SIB 61 16 21 (2021), G20 and others, energy management: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2021/MC-10205095-9999.pdf",
  "SIB122219": "BMW SIB 12 22 19 (rev. 2020), G20 PHEV and others, EME ground monitoring: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2020/MC-10179811-9999.pdf",
  "SIB610421": "BMW SIB 61 04 21 (2021), G20 and others, digital key: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2021/MC-10202469-9999.pdf",
  "SIBOIL19": "BMW SIB (2019), engine oil level message, G20/G07/G29 and others: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2019/MC-10159117-9999.pdf",
  "R21EA01": "BMW recall 21E-A01 bulletin (2024), G20 and others, dual storage battery: https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/tsbs/2024/MC-10250893-0001.pdf",
  "COMMUNITY": "community CC-ID list, E/F-series era, no BMW source cited, lists disagree with each other: https://www.tepte.com/bmw-cc-id-codes-list-and-possible-causes-id68.article (cross-checked against https://autoexplain.com/bmw-cc-id-code-list/ only by the search summary)"
 },
